+# Plan: joint return where both spouses have Form 1098-E interest (TY2025)
+
+Goal: decide whether the team understands enough to select an approach for
+handling a married-filing-jointly return on which each spouse has student-loan
+interest reported on their own 1098-E — and if not, what must be established first.
+No representation is selected and nothing is implemented here.
+
+## Actions (snapshot 0, plain language)
+
+1. Find out what the application does today with student-loan interest: how a
+   1098-E enters, whose it is, how answers are recorded, and how line 21 is computed.
+2. Find out what the application currently knows or assumes about filing status
+   and spouses (is there a "spouse" at all; is a joint return supported or refused).
+3. Pin down the 2025 tax rules for a joint return: combined $2,500 cap per return,
+   joint MAGI phase-out range, MFS exclusion, "qualified loan" / "legally obligated"
+   tests applied per spouse, dependency-claim bar, loans for a spouse as a student.
+4. Work out the concrete cases a joint return produces (each spouse with one or more
+   1098-Es, one spouse ineligible, combined interest over the cap, loan taken for
+   the other spouse, etc.) and which are new versus already handled.
+5. Identify the decisions the team must make (e.g., per-person vs per-return
+   attribution, how eligibility answers attach to each spouse, how explanation shows
+   both) and what evidence each decision needs — prototypes, tests, owner input.
+6. State readiness: can an approach be selected now, on what support, or what
+   must be established first.
